@node Introduction 
@chapter Introduction
Gtkdialog is a small utility program based on the GTK+ library. The
program mainly made for GUI development for shell scripts but can be
used with many other programming languages. The programmer can easily
create GUI not just for any shell script or UNIX command but for any
interpreted or compiled program capable to start child process and use
pipes.

@node Invoking the Program
@chapter Invoking the Program
@section Examples
Our first example program shows how to call the @command{gtkdialog}
from a BASH script. 

@example
#! /bin/bash

export MAIN_DIALOG='
 <vbox>
  <text>
    <label>This is a label...</label>
  </text>
  <hbox>
   <button ok></button>
   <button cancel></button>
  </hbox>
 </vbox>'

gtkdialog --program MAIN_DIALOG
@end example

This example uses a very plain way to open a dialog box. We store the
description of the dialog box in the @code{MAIN_DIALOG} environment
variable which is exported to the child processes with the BASH
@code{export} built-in. Then we call the @code{gtkdialog} program with
the @option{--program} option which is followed by the name of the
variable holding the dialog box description. It is simple and easy to
write BASH scripts in this manner.

A similar calling method can be used when user input is needed. The
@command{gtkdialog} send the state of the widgets to the standard
output when exiting and this is how we can get user input for the BASH
program. The  next example code show the reading process.
@example
#! /bin/bash

export DIALOG='
<vbox>
  <entry>
    <variable>ENTRY</variable>
  </entry>
  <hbox>
    <button ok></button>
    <button cancel></button>
  </hbox>
</vbox>'

I=$IFS; IFS=""
for STATEMENTS in  $(gtkdialog --program DIALOG); do
  eval $STATEMENTS
done
IFS=$I

if [ "$EXIT" = "OK" ]; then 
  echo "You entered: $ENTRY."
else
  echo "You pressed the Cancel button."
fi
@end example

In the example script we use the @code{for} built-in to go through the
list @code{gtkdialog} produced. Changing the field separator (IFS) is
a little bit disturbing but necessary since this is the only way to
protect the space characters in user input. 

In larger software projects it may be a good idea to break the code to
separate files. Since @code{gtkdialog} can read the description
program from file it is easy to write self executable programs with
it. This is how the next example constructed.

@example
#! /usr/local/bin/gtkdialog -f
<vbox>
  <checkbox>
    <label>This is a checkbox</label>
    <variable>CHECK1</variable>
  </checkbox>
  <checkbox>
    <label>Another one</label>
    <variable>CHECK2</variable>
  </checkbox>
  <button>
    <label>OK</label>
  </button>
</vbox>
@end example

When used in this fashion the state of the widgets can get from
the standard output of the script as usually.


@node Widgets
@chapter Widgets
The dialog description language is a simple XML like language capable 
to denote any complex dialog box containing widgets and boxes. 

Comments can be placed before or after the dialog and between widget
definitions using @code{<!-- comment -->}. Comments cannot be nested.
Comment markers inside a double-quoted string are treated as ordinary
text. Parser error line numbers include lines inside comments and
double-quoted strings and attributes.

Widgets are simple GUI elements such as buttons, entry fields, lists,
etc. Widget can have attributes, states and actions (@pxref{Actions}).

The widgets are grouped together with containers (@pxref{Containers}),
horizontal and vertical boxes or frames. Every widget should placed 
in one of the containers, no widgets can be alone for it is dangerous 
outside.

@menu
* Assistant::			Multi-page guided tasks
* About dialog::		Application information and credits
* Dialog::			Custom content and response buttons
* Drawing area::		Raster canvas and pointer actions
* Message dialog::		Standard messages and questions
* Icon view::			Selectable icon collections
* Cell view::			One compact model row
* Recent chooser::		Recently used resources
* Recent chooser menu::	Recent resources in a menu
* Scrollbars::			Standalone range positions
* Rulers::			Measured GTK2 ranges
* Curve editor::		Interactive transfer curves
* Scale and volume buttons::	Compact numeric controls
* Aspect frame::		Fixed-proportion content
* Static label::                Labels containing static text
* Multiline edit::              Editable text in a scrolled window
* Pushbutton::			Pushbutton widgets
* Pre-defined pushbuttons::	Pushbuttons with pixmap and text label
* Entry::			One line text entry widgets
* Checkbox::			Checkbox widgets
* Calendar::			Calendar date selection widget
* Link button::			Clickable URI widget
* Chooser::			Embedded file selection
* File chooser button::		Embedded file or folder selection
* Info bar::			Non-modal messages and responses
* Spinner::			Animated activity indicator
* XEmbed socket and plug::	External X11 application embedding
* Pixmap::			Static pixmap widgets
* Menus::                       Making menubars, menus and menuitems
* Trees::                       Complex widget for trees
@end menu

@c -----------------------------------------------------------------
@node Assistant
@section Assistant

The top-level @code{<assistant></assistant>} element creates a native GTK+ 2
multi-page assistant.  It replaces @code{<window>} as the program root and
cannot be nested inside another window.  Each direct child becomes one page.

Page widgets accept @code{page-title}, a Boolean @code{complete} state and
@code{page-type}: @code{content}, @code{intro}, @code{confirm},
@code{summary} or @code{progress}.  The assistant variable and
file output contain the zero-based current page number.  Defaults and inputs
select a page.

The default action signal is @code{apply}.  The @code{apply}, @code{cancel},
@code{close} and @code{prepare} signals may also be named explicitly.

@example
<assistant title="Setup">
  <vbox page-title="Welcome" page-type="intro" complete="true">
    <text><label>Welcome</label></text>
  </vbox>
  <vbox page-title="Ready" page-type="confirm" complete="true">
    <text><label>Ready to finish</label></text>
  </vbox>
  <variable>CURRENT_PAGE</variable>
  <action>exit:Apply</action>
</assistant>
@end example

The widget requires GTK+ 2.10 or later.

@c -----------------------------------------------------------------
@node About dialog
@section About dialog

The top-level @code{<aboutdialog></aboutdialog>} element creates a native
GTK+ 2 application-information dialog.  It replaces the normal
@code{<window>} root, cannot be nested, and does not accept child widgets.

Standard writable GtkAboutDialog properties are tag attributes.  Common
properties include @code{program-name}, @code{version}, @code{comments},
@code{copyright}, @code{website}, @code{website-label}, @code{license},
@code{wrap-license}, @code{translator-credits} and @code{logo-icon-name}.
Changing @code{program-name} also lets GTK form the native window title.

Each @code{<item>} adds one person to the credits.  Its optional
@code{role} attribute accepts @code{author} (the default),
@code{documenter} or @code{artist}.

The variable and file output contain the latest response.  The native Close
button normally returns @code{cancel}; closing through the window manager
returns @code{delete-event}.  The default action signal is @code{response},
and its value is updated before actions run.  Responses do not implicitly
exit or close a launched dialog, so use an explicit @code{exit} or
@code{closewindow} action.

@example
<aboutdialog program-name="GTKDialog GTK2" version="0.9.1"
             comments="Build GTK+ 2 interfaces from XML.">
  <item role="author">GTKDialog contributors</item>
  <item role="documenter">Documentation contributors</item>
  <variable>ABOUT_RESPONSE</variable>
  <action>exit:response</action>
</aboutdialog>
@end example

The widget requires GTK+ 2.6 or later.  The @code{wrap-license} property
requires GTK+ 2.8; @code{program-name} is translated to the older GTK name
API when building against GTK+ 2.6 or 2.10.

@c -----------------------------------------------------------------
@node Dialog
@section Dialog

The top-level @code{<dialog></dialog>} element creates a native GTK+ 2
dialog with separate content and action areas.  It replaces the normal
@code{<window>} root and cannot be nested inside another window.

Direct children without a @code{response} tag attribute are placed in the
content area.  A direct @code{<button>} child whose @code{response} attribute
is a response name or integer is placed in the action area.  Named responses
include @code{ok}, @code{cancel}, @code{close}, @code{yes}, @code{no},
@code{apply}, @code{help}, @code{accept} and @code{reject}.

The @code{default-response} tag attribute selects the default action button.
The dialog variable and file output contain the latest response.  The default
action signal is @code{response}, and the value is updated before actions run.
A response does not implicitly exit or close the dialog; use an explicit
@code{exit} or @code{closewindow} action.

@example
<dialog title="Settings" default-response="ok">
  <vbox><text><label>Apply these settings?</label></text></vbox>
  <button response="cancel"><label>Cancel</label></button>
  <button response="ok"><label>Apply</label></button>
  <variable>DIALOG_RESPONSE</variable>
  <action>exit:response</action>
</dialog>
@end example

@c -----------------------------------------------------------------
@node Drawing area
@section Drawing area

The @code{<drawingarea></drawingarea>} element creates a native GTK+ 2 raster
canvas.  A file input loads an image directly.  A command input reads an image
filename from its first output line.  The @code{<default>} directive supplies
an initial image filename, and the variable contains the filename of the last
image loaded successfully.

The @code{scale-mode} tag attribute accepts @code{none}, @code{fit} or
@code{stretch}.  @code{none} draws at the original size from the top-left;
@code{fit} preserves the aspect ratio and centres the image; @code{stretch}
fills the canvas.  Scaled images use @code{bilinear} interpolation by default;
@code{nearest}, @code{tiles}, @code{bilinear} or @code{hyper} may be selected
with @code{interpolation}.  The @code{background} attribute accepts a GTK
colour specification.  Width and height directives define the canvas size;
without them a loaded image supplies its natural dimension, with a 320 by 180
blank fallback.

@example
<drawingarea scale-mode="fit" background="#20242a">
  <width>480</width><height>270</height>
  <input file>/tmp/preview.png</input>
  <output file>/tmp/saved-preview.png</output>
  <variable>PREVIEW_IMAGE</variable>
  <action signal="button-press-event">
    echo "$PTR_X,$PTR_Y button $PTR_BTN"
  </action>
</drawingarea>
@end example

@code{refresh} reloads the declared sources, @code{clear} removes the image,
and @code{save} writes the original unscaled raster using the filename
extension selected by the output target.  The @code{expose-event} signal is
emitted after the current raster has been drawn.  Normal button actions receive
the existing @code{PTR_X}, @code{PTR_Y}, @code{PTR_BTN} and related pointer
environment values.  The drawing area owns its source and scaled pixbufs and
releases both with the widget.

@c -----------------------------------------------------------------
@node Message dialog
@section Message dialog

The top-level @code{<messagedialog></messagedialog>} element creates a native
GTK+ 2 message dialog.  Like @code{<assistant>}, it replaces the normal
@code{<window>} root and cannot be nested inside another window.

The @code{message-type} attribute accepts @code{info}, @code{warning},
@code{question}, @code{error} or @code{other}.  The @code{buttons} attribute
accepts @code{none}, @code{ok}, @code{close}, @code{cancel}, @code{yes-no} or
@code{ok-cancel}.  A @code{secondary-text} attribute adds explanatory text,
and @code{default-response} selects the button activated by Enter.  The
@code{<label>} directive supplies the primary message; use the normal
@code{title} tag attribute for the window title.

The variable and file output contain the latest named response, such as
@code{yes}, @code{no}, @code{ok} or @code{cancel}.  The default action signal
is @code{response}, and the variable is updated before its actions run.  A
response does not implicitly exit gtkdialog or close a launched dialog; use
the normal @code{exit} or @code{closewindow} action explicitly.

@example
<messagedialog title="Confirm" message-type="question"
               buttons="yes-no" default-response="yes"
               secondary-text="The project remains GTK2-only.">
  <label>Continue?</label>
  <variable>ANSWER</variable>
  <action>echo "$ANSWER"</action>
  <action>exit:response</action>
</messagedialog>
@end example

@c -----------------------------------------------------------------
@node Icon view
@section Icon view

The @code{<iconview></iconview>} element creates a native GTK+ 2 icon view.
Each @code{<item>} supplies a label and may specify a GTK stock icon,
an @code{icon-name} from the current icon theme, or an @code{image-name} or
@code{image-file}.
File and command inputs append one text-only item per line.  With
@code{input-mode="image"}, each line is loaded as an image filename and is
also used as the item's value.  The normal GtkIconView property
@code{text-column="-1"} hides item labels without changing those values.

The variable contains the selected label.  In @code{multiple} selection mode,
selected labels are separated by newlines.  A default selects the matching
label.  The @code{clear} action removes all items, @code{removeselected}
removes selected items, and @code{save} writes every current label to the
output file.

@example
<iconview selection-mode="single" icon-size="48" columns="3">
  <item stock="gtk-directory">Projects</item>
  <item stock="gtk-preferences">Settings</item>
  <variable>SELECTED_ICON</variable>
  <default>Projects</default>
  <action>echo "$SELECTED_ICON"</action>
</iconview>
@end example

For an image-only view populated from a file, use for example:

@example
<iconview input-mode="image" text-column="-1" icon-size="96">
  <input file>/tmp/image-list</input>
  <variable>SELECTED_IMAGE</variable>
</iconview>
@end example

The default action signal is @code{item-activated};
@code{selection-changed} may be named explicitly.  The widget requires
GTK+ 2.6 or later.

@c -----------------------------------------------------------------
@node Cell view
@section Cell view

The @code{<cellview></cellview>} element creates a native GTK+ 2 cell view
containing one model row.  It is a compact, non-interactive presentation of
text with an optional GTK stock icon, themed @code{icon-name}, or
@code{image-name}/@code{image-file}.  The @code{icon-size} attribute is a
positive pixel size and defaults to 24.  Set @code{use-markup="true"} to
interpret the text as Pango markup.

The @code{<default>} directive overrides @code{<label>}.  File and command
inputs replace the current text with their complete contents, and the
displayed text is exported as the widget value.  @code{refresh} restores the
declared image and reloads the text, @code{clear} removes both fields, and
@code{save} writes the current text to the output file.

@example
<cellview stock="gtk-dialog-info" icon-size="32" use-markup="true">
  <label>"<b>Ready</b>"</label>
  <input file>/tmp/application-status</input>
  <output file>/tmp/saved-status</output>
  <variable>STATUS</variable>
</cellview>
@end example

The object-valued model and tree-path-valued displayed row are owned by
gtkdialog rather than exposed as textual properties.  GtkCellView has no
type-specific default signal; normal GtkWidget signals remain available.
The widget requires GTK+ 2.6 or later.

@c -----------------------------------------------------------------
@node Recent chooser
@section Recent chooser

The @code{<recentchooser></recentchooser>} element creates an embeddable
GTK+ 2 chooser backed by the desktop's recent-resource manager.  It displays
the current user's recent resources; @code{<item>} does not populate this
shared history.

The variable and output file contain the selected URI.  When the standard
@code{select-multiple} property is true, selected URIs are separated by
newlines.  Defaults and file or command inputs accept the same format.
The @code{clear} and @code{removeselected} actions unselect resources without
removing them from desktop history.

Common GtkRecentChooser properties include @code{local-only},
@code{show-private}, @code{show-not-found}, @code{show-tips},
@code{show-icons}, @code{select-multiple}, @code{limit} and
@code{sort-type}.  The custom @code{filter-name}, @code{filter-pattern} and
@code{filter-mime-type} attributes add one optional recent-resource filter;
pattern and MIME lists are separated with commas or semicolons.

@example
<recentchooser local-only="true" show-not-found="false" limit="12"
               filter-name="Documents"
               filter-pattern="*.txt;*.md;*.pdf">
  <variable>RECENT_URI</variable>
  <action>echo "$RECENT_URI"</action>
</recentchooser>
@end example

The default action signal is @code{item-activated};
@code{selection-changed} may be named explicitly.  The widget requires
GTK+ 2.10 or later.

@c -----------------------------------------------------------------
@node Recent chooser menu
@section Recent chooser menu

The @code{<recentchoosermenu></recentchoosermenu>} element creates a labelled
menu item whose submenu is a native GTK+ 2 recent chooser menu.  It is valid
where an ordinary menu item is accepted, including a @code{<menu>} in a menu
bar or popup menu.  The enclosing item owns the chooser and its filters.

The @code{<label>} directive names the submenu entry.  The recent chooser
properties @code{local-only}, @code{show-private}, @code{show-not-found},
@code{show-tips}, @code{show-icons}, @code{limit} and @code{sort-type} are
available, together with the menu-specific @code{show-numbers} property.
The optional @code{filter-name}, @code{filter-pattern} and
@code{filter-mime-type} attributes use the same syntax as
@code{<recentchooser>}.  A recent chooser menu has a single current URI and
does not support multiple selection.

@example
<menu label="File">
  <recentchoosermenu show-numbers="true" limit="10"
                     filter-pattern="*.txt;*.md;*.pdf">
    <label>Recent documents</label>
    <variable>RECENT_URI</variable>
    <action>echo "$RECENT_URI"</action>
  </recentchoosermenu>
</menu>
@end example

Defaults and file or command inputs establish a logical current URI without
marking a visible menu entry.  Activating an entry replaces that value with
the URI reported by GTK+.  @code{refresh}, @code{save}, @code{clear} and
@code{removeselected} do not alter the desktop recent-resource history.  The
default signal is @code{item-activated}; @code{selection-changed} may be named
explicitly for GTK+ operations that emit it.  The widget requires GTK+ 2.10
or later.

@c -----------------------------------------------------------------
@node Scrollbars
@section Scrollbars

The @code{<hscrollbar></hscrollbar>} and
@code{<vscrollbar></vscrollbar>} elements create standalone GTK+ 2
scrollbars.  They expose a numeric position without creating a surrounding
scrolled window.  The custom @code{range-min}, @code{range-max},
@code{range-step}, @code{range-page}, @code{range-page-size} and
@code{range-value} attributes construct their private GtkAdjustment.

The page size controls the thumb extent and reduces the greatest usable
position to @code{range-max - range-page-size}.  Bounds and all values must
be complete finite numbers, the increments must be positive, and page size
must fit inside the range.  The standard GtkRange @code{inverted} property
is also available.

Variables, defaults, command or file inputs and output files contain the
numeric position in a locale-independent decimal representation.
@code{refresh} reloads the inputs and @code{save} writes the current value.
File monitoring and automatic refresh use the common widget contract.

@example
<hscrollbar range-min="0" range-max="100" range-step="1"
            range-page="10" range-page-size="20" range-value="35">
  <variable>SCROLL_POSITION</variable>
  <action>echo "$SCROLL_POSITION"</action>
</hscrollbar>
@end example

The default action signal is @code{value-changed}.  The GtkRange signals
@code{adjust-bounds}, @code{change-value} and @code{move-slider} may be
named explicitly.  Both widgets are available throughout GTK+ 2.

@c -----------------------------------------------------------------
@node Rulers
@section Rulers

The @code{<hruler></hruler>} and @code{<vruler></vruler>} elements expose
GTK+ 2 horizontal and vertical rulers.  Their writable native
@code{lower}, @code{upper}, @code{position} and @code{max-size} properties
describe the displayed range.  The native @code{metric} property accepts
@code{pixels}, @code{inches} or @code{centimeters}.

The exported variable, @code{default}, command and file input, and saved
output contain the current position as a locale-independent number.
@code{refresh} reloads input, while @code{clear} moves the marker to the
current lower bound.  File monitoring and applicable common state and signal
actions are supported.  GtkRuler has no type-specific action signal, so an
action must name a common GtkWidget signal explicitly.

@example
<hruler lower="0" upper="100" position="35"
        max-size="100" metric="pixels">
  <variable>HORIZONTAL_POSITION</variable>
</hruler>
<vruler lower="0" upper="30" position="12"
        max-size="30" metric="centimeters">
  <variable>VERTICAL_POSITION</variable>
</vruler>
@end example

GtkRuler is deprecated by GTK, but remains a working GTK+ 2 widget and is
provided for GTK2-native drawing and measurement layouts.

@c -----------------------------------------------------------------
@node Scale and volume buttons
@section Scale and volume buttons

The @code{<scalebutton></scalebutton>} element creates a compact GTK+ 2
scale button.  Its @code{range-min}, @code{range-max}, @code{range-step} and
@code{range-value} attributes define the adjustment.  The optional
@code{icons} attribute is a comma- or semicolon-separated list of themed icon
names.  @code{icon-size} accepts a GTK icon-size number.

The @code{<volumebutton></volumebutton>} element uses GTK's standard volume
adjustment and icons.  Both widgets accept @code{digits} (0 through 16) to
control exported formatting.  Their variables, defaults, inputs and output
files contain a numeric value.  @code{clear} and @code{removeselected} set
the value to the adjustment minimum.

@example
<scalebutton range-min="0" range-max="10" range-step="0.5"
             range-value="4" digits="1">
  <variable>ZOOM</variable>
  <action>echo "$ZOOM"</action>
</scalebutton>
<volumebutton digits="2">
  <variable>VOLUME</variable>
  <default>0.75</default>
</volumebutton>
@end example

The default and explicit action signal is @code{value-changed}.  Both widgets
require GTK+ 2.12 or later.

@c -----------------------------------------------------------------
@node Aspect frame
@section Aspect frame

The @code{<aspectframe></aspectframe>} element creates a native GTK+ 2 aspect
frame and requires exactly one direct child.  The @code{xalign} and
@code{yalign} attributes place that child from 0 through 1.  A positive
@code{ratio} specifies width divided by height.  When @code{obey-child} is
true, GTK derives the ratio from the child's request; when false, the declared
ratio is enforced.

The label directive, variable, default, first line of input and file output
use the frame label.  @code{clear} and @code{removeselected} clear that label.

@example
<aspectframe xalign="0.5" yalign="0.5" ratio="1.7778"
             obey-child="false">
  <text width-request="320" height-request="180">
    <label>16:9 content</label>
  </text>
  <label>Preview</label>
  <variable>ASPECT_LABEL</variable>
</aspectframe>
@end example

The widget is available throughout GTK+ 2.

@c -----------------------------------------------------------------
@node Static label 
@section Static label
Label is a static text widget created with @code{<text></text>} 
tag. 

The text in a static label, can be set with the
@code{<label>@var{string}</label>} or the 
@code{<input file>@var{filename}</input>} expression.

The historical label is not placed in a scrolled window.  Set
@code{scrollable="true"} to opt into a GTK+ 2 @code{GtkScrolledWindow}.
The @code{width} and @code{height} attributes or directives set its minimum
size, which defaults to 200 by 100 pixels.  @code{hscrollbar-policy} and
@code{vscrollbar-policy} accept 0 (always), 1 (automatic) or 2 (never), and
@code{shadow-type} accepts the GTK+ 2 numeric values 0 through 4.

For a scrolling text label, @code{scroll-to-bottom="true"} keeps newly added
content visible while the view is already at the bottom.  If the user scrolls
up, refreshes preserve the current distance from the bottom.

@example
<text scrollable="true" scroll-to-bottom="true"
      width="320" height="120" xalign="0">
  <variable>LOG</variable>
  <input file>/path/to/log</input>
</text>
@end example

@c -----------------------------------------------------------------
@node Multiline edit
@section Multiline edit

The @code{<edit></edit>} element creates a GTK+ 2 @code{GtkTextView} in a
scrolled window.  It accepts initial @code{default} text, command and file
input, a variable, width and height directives, and file output.  The
@code{refresh}, @code{save}, @code{clear} and @code{removeselected} functions
operate on its text buffer.

Set @code{scroll-to-bottom="true"} for the same bottom-gravity behaviour as a
scrollable text label.  The scroll state and its signal handlers are owned by
the scrolled window and disappear when its window is closed, including
windows that are later launched again.

When gtkdialog was built with GtkSpell 2, @code{spell-check="true"} enables
inline spelling feedback and correction choices in the normal context menu.
Use @code{spell-language="en_US"}, for example, to select a dictionary;
otherwise GtkSpell uses the current locale.  @command{gtkdialog --version}
lists @code{GtkSpell 2} when this optional integration is available.

@c -----------------------------------------------------------------
@node Pushbutton
@section Pushbutton
The pushbutton is a clickable widget defined with the 
@code{<button></button>} tags. 

@subsection @code{<label></label>}
The @code{<label>@var{string}</label>} directive sets the text label of
the pushbutton. If no label and pixmap is given for the button,
gtkdialog will use @var{OK} as default.

@subsection @code{<input file></input>}
When creating buttons, the @command{<input file>@var{filename}</input>}
tag can be used to insert a pixmap into the button. The @var{filename}
must be a pixmap file. Gtkdialog will find this file with the
@command{locate} utility if necessary.

The pushbuttons can contain a label and a pixmap simultaneously. For
this you have to use the @code{<label></label>} and the 
@code{<input file></input>} as the next example shows:

@example
<button>
  <input file>/usr/share/GUIcompletion/button_save.xpm</input>
  <label>The label</label>
</button>
@end example

@subsection @code{<action></action>}
The @code{<action>@var{command}</action>} directive tells the gtkdialog
what to do, when the button is pressed. If the action is not given
explicitly the gtkdialog uses the default action, which is to exit
the program. In this case the printed variable list will 
contain a variable named @var{EXIT}, with the label of the activated 
button as value.

The buttons can handle more than one actions simultaneously. If there
are more @code{<action></action>} directive for the given button, they
will be executed one by one, in the right order.

@subsection @code{<sensitive></sensitive>}
The @code{<sensitive>@var{state}</sensitive>} directive specifies whether
the button initially accepts user input.  False, no, zero and the deprecated
value @code{disabled} make it insensitive without hiding it.  The historical
@code{<visible>disabled</visible>} spelling remains accepted as a deprecated
alias.  Initial visibility is instead controlled by the
@code{visible="false"} widget attribute.

@c -----------------------------------------------------------------
@node Pre-defined pushbuttons
@section Pre-defined pushbuttons
Gtkdialog supports a few pre-defined pushbuttons for simplify the creation 
of dialog boxes. The pre-defined buttons can be used the same manner the
normal pushbuttons, but they have a default text, pixmap and output variable.
Here is the list of available pre-defined pushbuttons:

@itemize @bullet
 @item @code{<button ok></button>}
 @item @code{<button cancel></button>}
 @item @code{<button help></button>}
 @item @code{<button yes></button>}
 @item @code{<button no></button>}
@end itemize

@c -----------------------------------------------------------------
@node Entry
@section Entry
The entry widget is a simple text input field, which can be used to
get a string from the user. 

@subsection @code{<default></default>}
The @code{<default>@var{string}</default>} directive sets the
default content of the entry.

@subsection Sensitivity and password display
The @code{<sensitive>@var{state}</sensitive>} directive specifies whether
the entry initially accepts user input.  Use the GTK+ 2 property attribute
@code{visibility="false"} to mask text in a password entry.  The historical
@code{<visible>disabled</visible>} and @code{<visible>password</visible>}
spellings remain accepted for compatibility, but the former controls
sensitivity rather than visibility.

@subsection @code{<action></action>}
The entry widgets are activating actions after their contents are
changed. 

@subsection Completion
The optional @code{completion="true"} attribute adds native GTK+ 2 entry
completion.  Each @code{<item>} supplies one suggestion.  File and command
inputs marked with @code{completion="true"} supply one suggestion per line;
unmarked inputs keep their existing behavior and set the entry text from
their first line.  The @code{completion-minimum-key-length} attribute sets
how many characters must be entered before suggestions appear and defaults
to one.

@example
<entry completion="true" completion-minimum-key-length="1">
  <item>Development</item>
  <item>Documentation</item>
  <input file completion="true">/tmp/suggestions</input>
  <variable>CATEGORY</variable>
</entry>
@end example

Refreshing the entry rebuilds the suggestion model.  Completion requires
GTK+ 2.4 or later.

@c -----------------------------------------------------------------
@node Checkbox
@section Checkbox
The checkbox is a simple widget with a label and a check mark which 
can be turned on and off by the user. Checkboxes are made with the
@code{<checkbox></checkbox>} directive.

@subsection @code{<label></label>}
The label is the text shown beside the check mark. Every checkbox
should have a label.

@subsection @code{<default></default>}
The initial state of the checkbox can be set by the 
@code{<default>@var{state}</default>} directive, where the @var{state}
can be either @code{yes} or @code{no}.

@subsection @code{<action></action>}
The @code{<action></action>} directive tells the gtkdialog what to do,
when the state of the checkbox is changed. As every widgets, the
checkbox can hold multiply actions which are executed serially in the
order they are written.

Actions of checkboxes can be written as conditional instructions with 
@command{if true} and @command{if false} prefixes as in the next 
example:

@example
<checkbox>
	<label>This is a checkbox...</label>
	<variable>CHECKBOX</variable>
	<action>echo Checkbox is $CHECKBOX now.</action>
	<action>if true enable:ENTRY</action>
	<action>if false disable:ENTRY</action>
</checkbox>
@end example

If an action using one of these legacy prefixes also has a
@code{condition} attribute, the explicit condition is evaluated last and its
result determines whether the action is executed.  This historical precedence
is retained for compatibility.

@subsection @code{<sensitive></sensitive>}
The @code{<sensitive>@var{state}</sensitive>} directive specifies whether
the checkbox initially accepts user input.  An insensitive checkbox remains
visible but is shaded and cannot be changed.  The historical
@code{<visible>disabled</visible>} spelling remains accepted as a deprecated
alias; initial visibility uses the @code{visible="false"} widget attribute.

@subsection @code{<variable></variable>}
The value of a checkbox can be @code{true} or @code{false} and depends 
only on its state.

The @code{activate} action function toggles a checkbox or toggle button.
For a radio button it selects the target without clearing an already active
member of its group.

@c -----------------------------------------------------------------
@node Calendar
@section Calendar

The @code{<calendar></calendar>} widget presents a native GTK+ 2 calendar.
Its variable and file output use the locale-independent
@code{YYYY-MM-DD} format.  The @code{<default>}, command
@code{<input>} and @code{<input file>} directives accept that same format.

An action without an explicit signal runs when the user selects a day.
The named signals @code{day-selected}, @code{day-selected-double-click}
and @code{month-changed} are also available.  Programmatic refresh and
initialisation do not run actions when
@code{block-function-signals="true"} is used.

The custom Boolean attributes @code{show-heading}, @code{show-day-names},
@code{no-month-change} and @code{show-week-numbers} control the standard
GTK+ calendar display flags.

@example
<calendar show-week-numbers="true">
  <variable>SELECTED_DATE</variable>
  <default>2026-09-12</default>
  <action>echo "$SELECTED_DATE"</action>
</calendar>
@end example

@c -----------------------------------------------------------------
@node Link button
@section Link button

The @code{<linkbutton></linkbutton>} widget displays a clickable URI using the
native GTK+ 2 link button.  The required @code{uri} tag attribute supplies the
initial target and @code{<label>} supplies optional display text.  Without a
label, GTK+ displays the URI.

A @code{<default>}, command @code{<input>} or @code{<input file>} can replace
the URI.  The widget variable and file output contain the current URI.  The
default action signal is @code{clicked}; GTK+ also opens the URI using the
desktop URI handler.

@example
<linkbutton uri="https://www.gtk.org/">
  <label>GTK website</label>
  <variable>GTK_URI</variable>
  <action>echo "$GTK_URI"</action>
</linkbutton>
@end example

The widget requires GTK+ 2.18 or later.

@c -----------------------------------------------------------------
@node Chooser
@section Chooser

The @code{<chooser></chooser>} widget embeds a GTK+ 2 open-file view directly
in a window.  Its variable and file output contain the selected local
pathname, or an empty string when nothing is selected.  For backward
compatibility, @code{<default>} names the initially displayed folder rather
than a selected file.  Command @code{<input>} and @code{<input file>} select a
pathname; the @code{default-file} tag attribute supplies an initial selection
and overrides both the folder and initial input selection.

The @code{fs-filters-mime} and @code{fs-filters} attributes contain
pipe-separated MIME types or shell patterns.  Every entry becomes a separate
filter.  The default action signal is @code{file-activated}; explicit chooser
signals include @code{selection-changed}, @code{update-preview} and
@code{current-folder-changed}.  The historical @code{when="type"} action
attribute remains an alias for @code{signal="type"}.

The @code{refresh}, @code{save}, @code{clear}, @code{removeselected} and
@code{fileselect} functions read, store, clear or replace the selection.

@example
<chooser default-file="/tmp/report.txt" fs-filters="*.txt|*.md">
  <default>/tmp</default>
  <variable>SELECTED_FILE</variable>
  <action>echo "$SELECTED_FILE"</action>
</chooser>
@end example

The widget remains in open-existing-file mode and requires GTK+ 2.4 or later.

@c -----------------------------------------------------------------
@node File chooser button
@section File chooser button

The @code{<filechooserbutton></filechooserbutton>} widget embeds a native
GTK+ 2 file or folder selector.  Its variable and file output contain the
selected local pathname, or an empty string when nothing is selected.
@code{<default>}, command @code{<input>} and @code{<input file>} select a
pathname.

The @code{action} tag attribute accepts @code{open}, the default, or
@code{select-folder}.  @code{title} and @code{current-folder} configure the
chooser.  One filter can be described with @code{filter-name}, a semicolon-
or comma-separated @code{filter-pattern} list and a similarly separated
@code{filter-mime-type} list.

The default action signal is @code{file-set}.  The @code{refresh},
@code{save}, @code{clear} and @code{removeselected} action functions read,
store or clear the selection.

@example
<filechooserbutton title="Select an image"
  filter-name="Images" filter-pattern="*.png;*.jpg">
  <variable>SELECTED_IMAGE</variable>
  <action>echo "$SELECTED_IMAGE"</action>
</filechooserbutton>
@end example

The widget requires GTK+ 2.6 or later.

@c -----------------------------------------------------------------
@node Info bar
@section Info bar

The @code{<infobar></infobar>} container presents non-modal information,
warnings, questions or errors using the native GTK+ 2 info bar.  The
@code{message-type} attribute accepts @code{info}, @code{warning},
@code{question}, @code{error} or @code{other}.

Direct children form its content.  A direct button with a @code{response}
tag attribute is placed in the action area.  Named responses include
@code{ok}, @code{cancel}, @code{close}, @code{yes}, @code{no}, @code{apply},
@code{help}, @code{accept} and @code{reject}; integer response identifiers
are also accepted.  @code{default-response} selects the default response.

The variable and file output contain the most recent response.  The default
action signal is @code{response}; the variable is updated before the action
runs.

@example
<infobar message-type="warning" default-response="ok">
  <text><label>Continue?</label></text>
  <button response="cancel"><label>Cancel</label></button>
  <button response="ok"><label>Continue</label></button>
  <variable>INFO_RESPONSE</variable>
</infobar>
@end example

The widget requires GTK+ 2.18 or later.

@c -----------------------------------------------------------------
@node Spinner
@section Spinner

The @code{<spinner></spinner>} widget displays the native animated GTK+ 2
activity indicator.  Its state and variable are Boolean: @code{true} animates
the spinner and @code{false} stops it.  The custom @code{active} tag attribute
sets the initial state.

The @code{<default>}, command @code{<input>} and @code{<input file>}
directives also accept a Boolean state.  The @code{refresh} action rereads
configured input, @code{save} writes the current state, and @code{clear} or
@code{removeselected} stops the spinner.  A spinner has no default action
signal, but common widget signals such as @code{realize}, @code{show} and
pointer events remain available explicitly.  The @code{active_is_true} and
@code{active_is_false} action conditions inspect its current state.

@example
<spinner active="true">
  <variable>BUSY</variable>
</spinner>
@end example

The widget requires GTK+ 2.20 or later.

@c -----------------------------------------------------------------
@node XEmbed socket and plug
@section XEmbed socket and plug

The @code{<socket></socket>} widget creates a direct GTK+ 2
@code{GtkSocket} that can host an external XEmbed client.  Its variable is
empty before realization and contains the unsigned decimal X11 window ID
afterward.  The ID is exported before a @code{realize} action runs.

The default action signal is @code{plug-added}; @code{plug-removed} can be
selected explicitly.  Gtkdialog keeps the socket alive after a client
disconnects so another client can attach.  It does not launch, own or stop the
external process.  Because command actions are synchronous, a long-running
client launched from an action must run in the background.

@example
<socket width-request="640" height-request="400">
  <variable>SOCKET_ID</variable>
  <action signal="realize">xterm -into "$SOCKET_ID" &</action>
  <action>echo "XEmbed client attached"</action>
  <action signal="plug-removed">echo "XEmbed client detached"</action>
</socket>
@end example

The widget requires GTK+ 2 with the X11 backend and an XEmbed-capable client.
It is not available as a GTK3 or Wayland integration.

The @code{<plug></plug>} top-level element is the client counterpart.  Its
@code{socket-id} attribute accepts the unsigned decimal ID of an existing
XEmbed socket.  It contains one normal gtkdialog widget hierarchy and uses
the existing window lifecycle, state, title input/output and general actions.
The default action signal is @code{embedded}.

@example
<plug socket-id="123456">
  <vbox>
    <text><label>Embedded gtkdialog interface</label></text>
  </vbox>
  <action>echo "gtkdialog plug attached"</action>
  <variable>EMBEDDED_DIALOG</variable>
</plug>
@end example

The socket ID must be known before the plug is parsed.  A host action can
therefore construct the plug program in a shell variable and start a second
gtkdialog process.  Destroying the plug removes only that client; gtkdialog
does not take ownership of the host process.

@c -----------------------------------------------------------------
@node Pixmap
@section Pixmap
The @code{<pixmap></pixmap>} defines a static pixmap widget. 

@subsection @code{<input file></input>}

The widget must have an input file defined with the 
@code{<input file>@var{filename}</input>} tags. The @var{filename} 
is the graphic image file for the pixmap. Gtkdialog will load this
file if it can be opened for read, or will try to find a file with
similar name (using the @code{locate} utility program) if the file
is unreadable.  

The next example defines a static pixmap:

@example
<pixmap>
  <input file>help.png</input>
</pixmap>
@end example

@c -----------------------------------------------------------------
@node Menus 
@section Menubar 
The @code{<menubar></menubar>} defines menu bar which can be placed 
as any other screen elements. In the menubar widget you have to create
menus with the @code{<menu></menu>} tag, and inside the menu
must be at least one menu item created by the
@code{<menuitem></menuitem>} tag.

The next example shows how to create a simple menubar with only one
menu:

@example
<menubar>
  <menu>
    <menuitem>
      <label>gtk-open</label>
    </menuitem>
    <menuitem>
      <label>gtk-save</label>
    </menuitem>
    <menuitem>
      <label>gtk-quit</label>
      <action>EXIT="quit"</action>
    </menuitem>
    <label>File</label>
  </menu>
</menubar>
@end example

@c -----------------------------------------------------------------
@node Trees
@section The tree
OK, it is not complete, but the next example seems to be just fine.

@example
<vbox>
  <tree>
    <label>Device    |Directory        |File         </label>
    <item>Hard drive |/usr/            |letter.tex    </item>
    <item>Hard drive |/etc/            |inittab       </item>
    <item>Hard drive |/etc/            |fstab         </item>
    <item>Network    |alpha:/home      |quota.user    </item>
    <item>Network    |alpha:/home      |quota.group   </item>
    <item>Network    |beta:/home/pipas |tmp           </item>
    <item>Network    |beta:/home/pipas |latexfiles    </item>
    <item>Network    |beta:/home/pipas |book          </item>
    <item>Network    |beta:/home/pipas |bin           </item>
    <item>Network    |beta:/home/pipas |documentation </item>
  </tree>
  <button ok></button>
</vbox>
@end example

When gtkdialog was configured with @code{--with-json-glib}, a tree can load
nested rows from a versioned JSON document.  Add @code{format="json"} to either
@code{<input>} or @code{<input file>}; without that explicit attribute the
historical pipe-separated format is unchanged.  The XML @code{<label>} still
declares the data columns.  Each JSON row requires a positional @code{values}
array and may contain mutually exclusive @code{icon-name} or @code{stock-id},
a Boolean @code{expanded} state, and a recursive @code{children} array.

@example
@{
  "version": 1,
  "rows": [
    @{
      "icon-name": "folder",
      "values": ["Projects", 2, true],
      "expanded": true,
      "children": [
        @{"stock-id": "gtk-file", "values": ["README", 1, false]@}
      ]
    @}
  ]
@}
@end example

JSON values may be strings, numbers, Booleans or @code{null}.  Missing trailing
values use model defaults; extra values reject the complete document.  Use a
decimal string for an unsigned 64-bit value that may exceed the signed JSON
integer range.  Input is validated completely before its first row is added,
and nesting is limited to 256 levels.

Use @code{<output file format="json">} to save the hierarchy, values, icons and
current expansion state in the same versioned format.  Normal
@code{<output file>} retains its historical flat format.  The JSON feature is
reported by @code{gtkdialog --version}; @file{examples/tree/tree_json} provides
a runnable example.

Text-rendered columns can opt into in-place editing with the
@code{column-editable} tag attribute.  Its pipe-separated Boolean values
correspond to the declared column labels; omitted values remain
non-editable, preserving the traditional tree behaviour.  An action with
@code{signal="cell-edited"} runs after the model has accepted the new value.
The existing @code{column-type} conversion rules also apply to edited
numeric cells.

@example
<tree column-editable="true|false">
  <variable>EDITABLE_TREE</variable>
  <label>Name|Owner</label>
  <item>Draft|Ada</item>
  <action signal="cell-edited">echo "$EDITABLE_TREE"</action>
</tree>
@end example

The @code{column-renderer} attribute can replace an individual text cell
with a native GTK+ 2 toggle renderer.  Use @code{toggle} for that column and
enable changes with the corresponding @code{column-editable} value.  The
default renderer remains @code{text}.  A changed toggle stores
@code{true}/@code{false} in a string column (or @code{1}/@code{0} in a
numeric column) and emits @code{cell-toggled} after updating the model.

@example
<tree column-renderer="text|toggle" column-editable="false|true"
      exported-column="1">
  <variable>SERVICES</variable>
  <label>Service|Enabled</label>
  <item>Indexer|false</item>
  <action signal="cell-toggled">echo "$SERVICES"</action>
</tree>
@end example

On GTK+ 2.6 and later, @code{column-renderer="progress"} displays a
column's numeric value as a native progress bar.  Displayed values are
rounded and limited to 0--100 without modifying the model, saved output or
exported tree variable.

@example
<tree column-renderer="text|progress" column-type="string|double"
      exported-column="1">
  <variable>JOBS</variable>
  <label>Job|Progress</label>
  <item>Compile|65.5</item>
</tree>
@end example

On GTK+ 2.6 and later, @code{column-renderer="combo"} edits a column from
a native drop-down list.  Declare each choice with an @code{<item>} whose
zero-based @code{combo-column} names the data column.  These choice items
are not tree rows.  Enable the corresponding @code{column-editable} value;
after a choice is stored, the existing @code{cell-edited} action signal is
emitted.  By default only declared choices can be selected.  Set the
corresponding pipe-separated @code{column-combo-has-entry} value to
@code{true} to permit an arbitrary typed value as well.

@example
<tree column-renderer="text|combo" column-editable="false|true"
      exported-column="1">
  <variable>TASKS</variable>
  <label>Task|State</label>
  <item combo-column="1">Planned</item>
  <item combo-column="1">Running</item>
  <item combo-column="1">Done</item>
  <item>Compile|Running</item>
  <action signal="cell-edited">echo "$TASKS"</action>
</tree>
@end example

On GTK+ 2.10 and later, @code{column-renderer="spin"} edits numeric values
with a native spin button.  The pipe-separated @code{column-spin-min},
@code{column-spin-max}, @code{column-spin-step} and
@code{column-spin-digits} attributes configure each data column.  Their
defaults are 0, 100, 1 and 0 respectively.  Enable editing with
@code{column-editable}; committing the value updates the normal tree model
and emits @code{cell-edited}.

@example
<tree column-renderer="text|spin" column-editable="false|true"
      column-type="string|int64" column-spin-min="0|0"
      column-spin-max="100|10" column-spin-step="1|1"
      column-spin-digits="0|0" exported-column="1">
  <variable>TASKS</variable>
  <label>Task|Retries</label>
  <item>Compile|2</item>
  <action signal="cell-edited">echo "$TASKS"</action>
</tree>
@end example

On GTK+ 2.10 and later, @code{column-renderer="accel"} displays and edits
keyboard accelerators in string columns.  Values use an XML-safe GTK+
accelerator spelling, for example @code{Primary+O} or
@code{Primary+Shift+O}.  Enable the corresponding @code{column-editable}
value.  A captured key combination emits
@code{accel-edited}; Backspace clears the cell and emits
@code{accel-cleared}.

@example
<tree column-renderer="text|accel" column-editable="false|true"
      exported-column="1">
  <variable>SHORTCUTS</variable>
  <label>Action|Shortcut</label>
  <item>Open|Primary+o</item>
  <item>Save|Primary+s</item>
  <action signal="accel-edited">echo "$SHORTCUTS"</action>
  <action signal="accel-cleared">echo "cleared"</action>
</tree>
@end example

On GTK+ 2.20 and later, @code{column-renderer="spinner"} displays a native
activity indicator.  The value in that model column controls whether the
indicator is active, using the same Boolean forms as a toggle renderer.  The
animation does not modify the model, saved output or exported tree variable,
and stops when its tree is destroyed.

@example
<tree column-renderer="text|spinner" exported-column="1">
  <variable>TASKS</variable>
  <label>Task|Busy</label>
  <item>Download metadata|true</item>
  <item>Validate cache|false</item>
</tree>
@end example

@section Embedded Colour Selection

The @code{<colorselection>} widget exposes a complete GTK+ 2
@code{GtkColorSelection} directly inside a window.  It complements the
compact @code{<colorbutton>} without changing that established tag.

The @code{has-palette} property shows GTK's shared colour palette and
@code{has-opacity-control} shows the alpha controls.  Its exported value and
@code{default}, command input, file input and saved output use the existing
colour-button format: @code{#rrggbb}, or @code{#rrggbb|alpha} when opacity is
enabled.  Alpha is a decimal integer in the range 0--65535.

The default action signal is the native @code{color-changed} signal.  The
selector also supports refresh, save, file monitoring, width and height size
requests, and all applicable common widget actions.

@example
<colorselection has-opacity-control="true" has-palette="true">
  <default>#4f81bd|49152</default>
  <variable>ACCENT_COLOUR</variable>
  <action>echo "$ACCENT_COLOUR"</action>
</colorselection>
@end example

@section Embedded Font Selection

The @code{<fontselection>} widget exposes a complete GTK+ 2
@code{GtkFontSelection} directly inside a window.  It complements the compact
@code{<fontbutton>} without changing that established tag.

Its exported value, @code{default}, @code{font-name}, command input, file
input and saved output use a complete Pango font description such as
@code{Sans Bold 12}.  The @code{preview-text} property changes the sample text
shown by the native selector.  The selected font must be available to the
active GTK display.

GtkFontSelection has no dedicated change signal in GTK+ 2.  Gtkdialog
therefore provides the logical @code{font-changed} signal as this widget's
default action signal.  It is emitted when the family, face or size changes,
and by refresh only when the resulting font value differs.  The selector also
supports save, file monitoring, width and height size requests, and all
applicable common widget actions.

@example
<fontselection preview-text="GTKDialog preview">
  <default>Sans Bold 12</default>
  <variable>INTERFACE_FONT</variable>
  <action>echo "$INTERFACE_FONT"</action>
</fontselection>
@end example

@section HSV Selector

The @code{<hsv>} tag exposes the GTK+ 2 @code{GtkHSV} colour component
selector.  Its value is written as three locale-independent numbers in the
inclusive 0 to 1 range, separated by vertical bars:
@code{hue|saturation|value}.  The same representation is used by
@code{default}, command and file input, the exported variable and saved
output.

The @code{hsv-size} and @code{ring-width} tag attributes configure the native
selector geometry.  Ring width must be positive and less than half of the
overall size.  The native @code{changed} signal is the default action signal.
The widget supports refresh, save, clear, file monitoring and applicable
common widget actions; clear resets the value to @code{0|0|1}.

@example
<hsv hsv-size="240" ring-width="28">
  <variable>HSV_VALUE</variable>
  <default>0.58|0.75|0.9</default>
  <action>echo "$HSV_VALUE"</action>
</hsv>
@end example

@section Curve Editor

The @code{<curve>} tag exposes the GTK+ 2 @code{GtkCurve} transfer-curve
editor.  This widget is deprecated only because it was not carried forward
after GTK+ 2; it remains part of the GTK2 ABI and is intentionally available
in this GTK2-only implementation.

Its value is a sequence of locale-independent Y values separated by vertical
bars.  @code{default}, command and file input accept between 2 and 4096
samples.  The exported variable and saved output contain the number selected
by the @code{samples} tag attribute, or the input vector length when that
attribute is omitted.  Samples include both endpoints of the X range.

The @code{curve-type} attribute accepts @code{linear}, @code{spline} or
@code{free}; @code{min-x}, @code{max-x}, @code{min-y} and @code{max-y} set
finite axis ranges, and @code{gamma} creates an initial positive gamma curve.
A default or input vector takes precedence over gamma.  If a curve type is
explicitly requested, it is applied after that initial vector.

The opt-in @code{controls="true"} attribute wraps the graph in the native
GTK+ 2 @code{GtkGammaCurve}.  It adds the original spline, linear, free,
gamma and reset controls without changing the value representation or the
default appearance of existing @code{<curve>} descriptions.

The logical default @code{changed} action is emitted when the user finishes
an edit with the pointer.  The native @code{curve-type-changed} signal is also
available explicitly, including changes made with the optional controls.  The
widget supports refresh, save, clear, file monitoring and applicable common
widget actions.  Clear restores the native straight diagonal over the
configured range.

@example
<curve controls="true" curve-type="spline" min-y="0" max-y="1" samples="5">
  <variable>TRANSFER</variable>
  <default>0|0.1|0.5|0.9|1</default>
  <action>echo "$TRANSFER"</action>
</curve>
@end example

@section Accelerator Label

The @code{<accellabel>} tag exposes GTK+ 2 @code{GtkAccelLabel}.  It displays
ordinary label text together with the keyboard accelerator installed on
another gtkdialog widget.  Set @code{accel-widget} to the variable name of
that target; a @code{<menuitem>} with @code{accel-key} and @code{accel-mods}
is the usual target.  The target may be written before or after the label in
the same window because gtkdialog completes the binding after constructing
the widget tree.

The exported value is the label text, not the accelerator suffix rendered by
GTK.  The widget supports @code{label}, @code{default}, command and file
input, file monitoring, refresh, save, clear and applicable common widget
actions.  Writable GtkAccelLabel and GtkLabel properties remain available as
tag attributes.  GtkAccelLabel has no type-specific default action signal;
explicit actions use the common GtkWidget signals.

@example
<menubar>
  <menu label="_File" use-underline="true">
    <menuitem label="Quit" accel-key="0x51" accel-mods="4">
      <variable>QUIT_ITEM</variable>
      <action>exit:Quit</action>
    </menuitem>
  </menu>
</menubar>
<accellabel accel-widget="QUIT_ITEM" xalign="0">
  <label>Quit command</label>
  <variable>QUIT_ACCEL_LABEL</variable>
</accellabel>
@end example

@section Directional Arrow

The decorative @code{<arrow>} tag exposes GTK+ 2 @code{GtkArrow}.  Its
@code{arrow-type} property accepts @code{up}, @code{down}, @code{left},
@code{right} or @code{none}; @code{shadow-type} controls the native relief.
Alignment and padding use the inherited @code{xalign}, @code{yalign},
@code{xpad} and @code{ypad} GtkMisc properties.

An optional variable exports an empty value and names the arrow for common
show, hide, enable, disable, state-condition and explicit GtkWidget signal
actions.  The widget has no type-specific input, output or default signal.

@example
<hbox homogeneous="true">
  <arrow arrow-type="left" shadow-type="in"></arrow>
  <arrow arrow-type="right" shadow-type="out"></arrow>
</hbox>
@end example

@section Notification-area Status Icon

The @code{<statusicon>} tag creates a GTK+ 2 @code{GtkStatusIcon}.  GTK status
icons are not window children, so gtkdialog owns each one through a zero-size
logical widget in the containing window.  Closing or relaunching that window
therefore destroys or recreates the corresponding icon without changing the
general variable and action model.

Use @code{icon-name} for a themed icon, @code{stock-id} for a GTK stock icon,
or @code{image-file} for a file.  Defaults, command/file input, the exported
variable and saved output use @code{icon:name}, @code{stock:name} or
@code{file:path}; a bare value is an icon name.  A @code{label} is a fallback
tooltip, while @code{tooltip-text}, @code{tooltip-markup}, @code{title} and
@code{blinking} expose the corresponding GTK2 facilities.

The default signal is @code{activate}.  Explicit
@code{button-press-event}, @code{button-release-event}, @code{popup-menu} and
@code{size-changed} actions follow the native status-icon signals.  A
@code{context-menu} names a @code{<popupmenu>} program and GTK positions that
menu at the notification icon.  Common show, hide, enable, disable, refresh,
save, clear, activate and state-condition actions operate through the logical
widget.  The feature requires GTK+ 2.10 or later.

@example
<statusicon icon-name="dialog-information"
            tooltip-text="GTKDialog is running"
            context-menu="TRAY_MENU">
  <variable>TRAY_ICON</variable>
  <action>presentwindow:MAIN_DIALOG</action>
</statusicon>
@end example

@c -----------------------------------------------------------------
@node Containers
@chapter Containers

@section Viewport

The @code{<viewport>} container exposes a GTK+ 2 @code{GtkViewport} and
requires exactly one direct child.  It is useful when the scrolling bridge
must be explicit rather than inserted automatically by
@code{<scrolledwindow>}.

When a viewport is placed directly in a @code{<scrolledwindow>}, GTK+ connects
the viewport to the surrounding horizontal and vertical adjustments.  No
additional viewport is created.  The standard @code{shadow-type} property
accepts values such as @code{none}, @code{in}, @code{out},
@code{etched-in} and @code{etched-out}.

The @code{<variable>} directive optionally names the container for common
visibility and sensitivity functions and exports an empty value.  The
@code{<width>} and @code{<height>} directives set its size request.  A
viewport has no type-specific input or output contract; its child keeps its
normal value and actions.  The object-valued @code{hadjustment} and
@code{vadjustment} properties are managed internally and are not accepted as
textual properties.

@example
<scrolledwindow hscrollbar-policy="never"
  vscrollbar-policy="automatic">
  <viewport shadow-type="in">
    <vbox>
      <text><label>Scrollable content</label></text>
      <text height-request="500"><label>Long child</label></text>
    </vbox>
    <variable>VIEWPORT</variable>
  </viewport>
</scrolledwindow>
@end example

@section Grid

The @code{<grid>} container is a GTK+ 2 GtkTable under a distinct XML name,
so the existing @code{<table>} data widget remains compatible.  It arranges
direct child widgets in rows and columns.  The @code{rows} and @code{columns}
attributes set the initial dimensions; children without explicit positions
are placed from left to right in XML order and wrap after @code{columns}.

Direct children accept zero-based @code{row} and @code{column} positions,
positive @code{row-span} and @code{column-span} values, and pixel
@code{x-padding} and @code{y-padding}.  The @code{x-options} and
@code{y-options} attributes combine @code{expand}, @code{shrink} and
@code{fill}, separated by spaces, commas or @code{|}; @code{none} selects no
options.  Their default is @code{expand|fill}.  Standard GtkTable properties
such as @code{row-spacing}, @code{column-spacing} and @code{homogeneous} are
also available.

@example
<grid rows="2" columns="2" row-spacing="8" column-spacing="12">
  <text row="0" column="0" x-options="fill" y-options="none">
    <label>Name</label>
  </text>
  <entry row="0" column="1"><variable>NAME</variable></entry>
  <hbuttonbox row="1" column="0" column-span="2" layout-style="end">
    <button cancel></button>
    <button ok></button>
  </hbuttonbox>
</grid>
@end example

@section Alignment

The @code{<alignment>} container positions and optionally scales one direct
child widget within the space allocated to it.  It requires exactly one
direct child; use an hbox or vbox when several widgets must be aligned as a
group.

The @code{xalign} and @code{yalign} tag attributes select the child's
horizontal and vertical position.  Values range from @code{0} at the left or
top through @code{0.5} at the centre to @code{1} at the right or bottom.
The @code{xscale} and @code{yscale} attributes select how much of the
available extra space the child receives, from @code{0} for its requested
size to @code{1} for all available space.  GTK+ 2.4 and later also provide
@code{top-padding}, @code{bottom-padding}, @code{left-padding} and
@code{right-padding}.

@example
<alignment xalign="1" yalign="1" xscale="0" yscale="0"
  right-padding="12" bottom-padding="12">
  <button ok></button>
</alignment>
@end example

@section Horizontal and vertical button boxes

The @code{<hbuttonbox>} and @code{<vbuttonbox>} containers arrange related
buttons using the standard GTK+ 2 button-box layout.  They preserve the XML
order of their direct children.  Use @code{layout-style} with
@code{default}, @code{spread}, @code{edge}, @code{start}, @code{end} or
@code{center} to select their placement.  The @code{center} style requires
GTK+ 2.12 or later.  Omitting the attribute retains the toolkit's configured
default.

A direct child with @code{secondary="true"} is placed separately from the
main group according to the selected GTK+ layout.  This is commonly used for
a Help button beside a group of confirmation buttons.

@example
<hbuttonbox layout-style="end" spacing="8">
  <button secondary="true">
    <label>Help</label>
  </button>
  <button cancel></button>
  <button ok></button>
</hbuttonbox>
@end example

@section Toolbars and tool items

GTK+ 2.4 and later provide the @code{<toolbar>} container.  Its direct
children must be @code{<toolbutton>}, @code{<toggletoolbutton>},
@code{<radiotoolbutton>}, @code{<menutoolbutton>} or
@code{<toolitem>} or @code{<separatortoolitem>} widgets, and their XML order
is retained.  The
toolbar's @code{toolbar-style}
attribute accepts @code{icons}, @code{text}, @code{both} or
@code{both-horizontal}.  Its @code{orientation} is @code{horizontal} or
@code{vertical}.  Omitting either attribute retains the GTK+ default.
When a vertical toolbar is packed into a vbox, use
@code{space-expand="true"} on the toolbar to allocate the available height;
otherwise GTK+ may place excess items behind its overflow arrow.

A toolbutton accepts a @code{<label>} directive and an ordinary
@code{<action>}, whose default signal is @code{clicked}.  GTK+ properties
such as @code{stock-id}, @code{icon-name}, @code{tooltip-text} and
@code{is-important} can be supplied as tag attributes when supported by the
installed GTK+ 2 version.  The @code{activate} action function also clicks a
named toolbutton.

A menutoolbutton combines a normal clickable tool button with a separate
arrow that opens a menu.  Put one or more @code{<menuitem>}, nested
@code{<menu>} or @code{<menuitemseparator>} widgets directly inside it,
followed by the tool button's directives.  Its main button uses the
@code{clicked} signal and can be invoked with @code{activate}; choosing an
entry runs that menu item's actions.  Menutoolbuttons require GTK+ 2.6 or
later.

An ordinary menu can begin with
@code{<menuitem tearoff="true"></menuitem>} to expose GTK+ 2's native
detachable-menu handle.  The opt-in item has no label, image, value or
accelerator.  Activating it toggles the detached menu window and may also run
normal action directives; menu descriptions without the attribute retain
their established behaviour.

A toggletoolbutton has a Boolean state.  Its @code{<default>} and
@code{<input>} directives set that state, and its variable value is
@code{true} or @code{false}.  Its default action signal is @code{toggled}.
The @code{activate} action function changes its state.

Radiotoolbuttons in one toolbar form an implicit exclusive group.
Each toolbar starts a new group, including toolbars built by imperative loops
or launched dialogs.  A radiotoolbutton uses the same Boolean directives,
output and @code{toggled} action signal as a toggletoolbutton.  Activating an
unselected member selects it without allowing the selected member to be
cleared by another activation.

A separator tool item draws a separator by default.  The GTK+ properties
@code{draw="false"} and @code{expand="true"} turn it into flexible space.

A general @code{<toolitem>} embeds exactly one ordinary gtkdialog widget in a
toolbar.  The embedded widget keeps its normal variable, input, output and
action behaviour; put interaction actions on that child.  The tool item can
be named separately for shared show, hide, enable and disable actions.  GTK+
properties such as @code{expand}, @code{homogeneous},
@code{visible-horizontal}, @code{visible-vertical} and @code{is-important}
can be supplied as tag attributes.  General tool items require GTK+ 2.4 or
later.

@example
<toolbar toolbar-style="both-horizontal">
  <toolbutton stock-id="gtk-new" is-important="true">
    <label>New</label>
    <action>echo new</action>
  </toolbutton>
  <separatortoolitem></separatortoolitem>
  <menutoolbutton stock-id="gtk-open">
    <menuitem>
      <label>Recent document</label>
      <action>echo recent</action>
    </menuitem>
    <menuitemseparator></menuitemseparator>
    <menu>
      <menuitem><label>Example one</label></menuitem>
      <menuitem><label>Example two</label></menuitem>
      <label>Examples</label>
    </menu>
    <label>Open</label>
    <action>echo open</action>
  </menutoolbutton>
  <separatortoolitem></separatortoolitem>
  <toolitem expand="true" homogeneous="false">
    <entry>
      <default>Search toolbar</default>
      <variable>TOOLBAR_SEARCH</variable>
    </entry>
  </toolitem>
  <toggletoolbutton stock-id="gtk-bold">
    <label>Bold</label>
    <variable>BOLD</variable>
  </toggletoolbutton>
  <radiotoolbutton stock-id="gtk-justify-left">
    <label>Left</label>
  </radiotoolbutton>
  <radiotoolbutton stock-id="gtk-justify-center">
    <label>Center</label>
  </radiotoolbutton>
  <separatortoolitem></separatortoolitem>
  <toolbutton stock-id="gtk-quit" is-important="true">
    <label>Quit</label>
    <action>exit:Quit</action>
  </toolbutton>
</toolbar>
@end example

@section Size groups

GTK+ 2.4 and later can give unrelated widgets the same requested width,
height or both without changing the container hierarchy.  Give each member
the same @code{size-group} tag attribute.  The optional
@code{size-group-mode} is @code{horizontal}, @code{vertical} or @code{both};
it defaults to @code{both}.

Size-group names are local to one constructed window, including one
execution of a launched window.  The first occurrence of a name selects its
mode.  A later member using the same name with another mode produces a
warning and retains the first mode.

@example
<hbox>
  <text size-group="field-labels" size-group-mode="horizontal">
    <label>Name</label>
  </text>
  <entry></entry>
</hbox>
<hbox>
  <text size-group="field-labels" size-group-mode="horizontal">
    <label>E-mail address</label>
  </text>
  <entry></entry>
</hbox>
@end example

@section Horizontal and vertical panes

The @code{<hpaned>} and @code{<vpaned>} containers divide an area into
two adjustable panes.  An hpaned places its first direct child on the
left and its second direct child on the right.  A vpaned places the first
child above the second child.  Both containers require exactly two direct
child widgets.

The @code{position} tag attribute sets the initial divider position in
pixels from the left or top edge.  If a @code{<variable>} is given, its
value is the current divider position.  The same numeric value can be set
with @code{<default>} or loaded with @code{<input>} and written with
@code{<output file>} by a @code{save} action.

The paned tag accepts @code{child1-resize}, @code{child1-shrink},
@code{child2-resize} and @code{child2-shrink} attributes.
The resize attributes control whether the corresponding pane changes size
when the complete paned widget is resized.  The shrink attributes control
whether the divider may make it smaller than the child's requested size.
By default the first pane does not resize, the second pane does, and both
panes may shrink, matching GTK+ 2.

@example
<hpaned position="180" child1-resize="false" child1-shrink="false"
  child2-resize="true" child2-shrink="true">
  <vbox>
    <text><label>Navigation</label></text>
  </vbox>
  <vbox>
    <text><label>Contents</label></text>
  </vbox>
  <variable>DIVIDER</variable>
</hpaned>
@end example

@c -----------------------------------------------------------------
@node Actions
@chapter Actions
When the user changes the state of a widget, gtkdialog checks if there
is something to do with it. If the tampered widget have one or more
actions, the program will execute them for the new situation to be
handled.

Every widget can have multiply actions, a list of commands must be
executed when the widget changed. Gtkdialog executes the axtions in
the order they found in the dialog description program, so one can
write a complet program as a series of instructions.

@menu
* Action conditions:: Conditions that decide whether an action runs
* Start and exit::	Actions that starts programs and exits dialogs
* Widget manipulation:: Actions affect widgets
@end menu

@c -----------------------------------------------------------------
@node Action conditions
@section Action conditions

The optional @code{condition} attribute controls whether an action runs.
@code{active_is_true}/@code{active_is_false},
@code{sensitive_is_true}/@code{sensitive_is_false} and
@code{visible_is_true}/@code{visible_is_false} inspect the corresponding state
of the widget named by their argument.  @code{variable_is_true} and
@code{variable_is_false} inspect that widget's current textual value without
starting a shell or using an intermediate file.  @code{command_is_true} and
@code{command_is_false} read the first output line of a command, while
@code{file_is_true} and @code{file_is_false} read the first line of a file.

Boolean values are @code{true}, @code{yes} or a complete non-zero decimal
integer for true, and @code{false}, @code{no} or a complete decimal zero for
false.  Other values satisfy neither recognised condition.  Unknown conditions
are reported and ignored for historical compatibility; missing variables and
recognised but inapplicable conditions are false.  A final closing parenthesis,
when present, ends the argument and following text is ignored.  Without a
closing parenthesis, the rest of the attribute remains the argument.

Classic state-taking widget inputs use a different historical conversion.
For @code{button}/@code{togglebutton}, @code{checkbox}, @code{expander},
check/radio @code{menuitem}, @code{radiobutton} and @code{timer}, @code{true},
@code{yes}, or a numeric prefix whose value is exactly one enables the state;
every other value disables it.

@example
<action condition="variable_is_true(ENABLED)">refresh:RESULT</action>
@end example

@c -----------------------------------------------------------------
@node Start and exit
@section Start and exit

@subsection Start programs

If the action of a widget is created with the simple 
@code{<action>@var{command}</action>} directive, gtkdialog will execute
it in a subshell. That means it will start up @code{/bin/sh} to handle 
the operation. Here is how the subshell operation works:
@enumerate
 @item First gtkdialog updates the environment variables holds the
 state and value of the widgets. This is how the child process will
 know what is happening in the GUI called it.
 
 @item Next the include file is checked. If the gtkdialog started
 with the @option{-i @var{file}} option gtkdialog will ask the
 subshell to include the @var{file} before the execution of command.

 This strange method is needed for the action driven programs, where
 the subshell have to load the shell functions from the calling script.

 @item At the third step gtkdialog starts the command and waits for
 it to complete. (Commands usually can be run in the background by 
 writing a @code{&} as last character, so the subshell will not wait 
 the program to complete.)
@end enumerate

Shell input redirection can be used directly inside @code{<action>} and
shell @code{<input>} directives, for example
@code{<input>wc -l </etc/passwd</input>}. Gtkdialog invokes
@code{/bin/sh}; commands which require Bash must invoke it explicitly.

The @option{--event-driven @var{file}} option loads @var{file} with
@code{/bin/sh}, then starts a new gtkdialog process using the variables
exported by that file. The file must be valid POSIX shell and should define
data and functions without launching gtkdialog itself. It is also used as the
new process's include file when executing shell actions and inputs.
Options controlling the dialog source and presentation, such as
@option{--file}, @option{--program}, @option{--geometry},
@option{--space-expand}, @option{--space-fill} and @option{--styles}, are
preserved for the new process. Packing states accept only
@code{true}/@code{yes}/@code{1} or @code{false}/@code{no}/@code{0}; an invalid
state is ignored with a warning.

@subsection Exit dialog

The @code{Exit:@var{Value}} command exits @command{gtkdialog}
immediately. The @var{Value} will be printed to standard output as the
value of the variable named @var{EXIT}.

@c -----------------------------------------------------------------
@node Widget manipulation
@section Widget manipulation

@subsection @code{Closewindow:@var{Name}}
The command closes the named window opened by the @code{Launch:}
command. The program remain active if there are more windows active.

@subsection @code{Launch:@var{Name}}
The command opens a new window using the environment variable
@code{Widget}.

@subsection @code{Disable:@var{Name}}
The command disables the given widget if it is enabled. If the widget
is disabled when the command is activated, nothing happens.

The disabled widgets are insensitive to user actions, their shapes are
indicating they are temporary unavailable.

@subsection @code{Enable:@var{Name}}
The command enables the given widget if it is diabled. If the widget
is enabled nothing happens.

@subsection @code{Refresh:@var{Name}}
The command refresh the named widget. If the widget have one or more
input actions, they will be called by @command{gtkdialog}.

@subsection @code{Save:@var{Name}}
Some widgets can hold much data. (Currently only the edit widget
capable to perform this action.)

The @code{Save:} action will save the data found in the named widget
to the filename found in @code{<output file>} attribute.

